|~ What is declared under `::number`: the refusal of its
   constructor, and the names that run on a number. A name holds its
   page and its primitive, the parts of the args of its call are
   declared under it, each with the invariant a part keeps, and the refusals
   its primitive raises sit under it too. ~|

:number/construct
  |~~ Reads a number as itself, a finite double. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/PayloadNotNumberError
  |~~ `::number` reads its payload as the number it is; a payload of
      another kind is refused, and text is read by `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/PayloadNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string payload is refused; `parseJson` reads a number from text. ~~|
  ::law~(::number"5" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/PayloadNotNumberError ::string])

:number/NotFiniteError
  |~~ A number is a finite double: an answer brought under `::number`
      that left their range, a sum or a product too large, is refused
      here, whichever word computed it, and the trail names the step and
      the subject it ran on.
  ~~|

:number/NotFiniteError/refusesAProductPastTheRange
  |~~ A product past the range of doubles is refused at the step that made it. ~~|
  ::law~(1e308 | mul 10 | add 1 !| [type (/trail * /step)] | eq [::number/NotFiniteError [~(mul 10)]])

:number/add
  |~~ Adds a number to the subject: `a | add b` computes a + b. A vector
      adds element by element, `* add 1`, and sums by `sum`; a string is
      joined by `append`; two numbers one value holds add by `sumOf`,
      `{:x 10 :y 3} | sumOf /x /y`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/add/addsTheNumber
  |~~ `add` computes the sum. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | add 3 | eq 13)

:number/add/refusesAStringSubject
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the name. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | add 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/add/refusesArgsBeyondItsPlaces
  |~~ Args with more numbers than the call has places are refused. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | add 1 2 !| type | eq ::number/add/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:number/add/addend
  |~~ The number added to the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/add/addend/refusesAString
  |~~ A string addend is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | add "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/add/addend/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/add/addend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The addend is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/add/answer
  |~~ The subject and the addend added. ~~|
  ::number

:number/sub
  |~~ Subtracts a number from the subject, the minuend. A vector
      subtracts element by element, `* sub 1`; two numbers one value
      holds subtract by `subOf`, `{:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total
      /paid`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/sub/subtractsTheNumber
  |~~ `sub` computes the difference. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | sub 3 | eq 7)

:number/sub/refusesAStringSubject
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | sub 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/sub/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/sub/subtrahend
  |~~ The number subtracted from the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/sub/subtrahend/refusesAString
  |~~ A string subtrahend is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | sub "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The subtrahend is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/sub/answer
  |~~ The subject less the subtrahend. ~~|
  ::number

:number/mul
  |~~ Multiplies the subject by a number. A vector multiplies element by
      element, `* mul 2`; two numbers one value holds multiply by
      `mulOf`, `{:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/mul/multipliesByTheNumber
  |~~ `mul` computes the product. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | mul 3 | eq 30)

:number/mul/refusesAStringSubject
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | mul 2 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/mul/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/mul/multiplier
  |~~ The number the subject is multiplied by. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/mul/multiplier/refusesAString
  |~~ A string multiplier is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | mul "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError
  |~~ The multiplier is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/mul/answer
  |~~ The subject times the multiplier. ~~|
  ::number

:number/div
  |~~ Divides the subject, the dividend, by a number. A vector divides
      element by element, `* div 2`; two numbers one value holds divide
      by `divOf`, `{:sum 20 :count 4} | divOf /sum /count`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/div/dividesByTheNumber
  |~~ `div` computes the quotient. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | div 2 | eq 5)

:number/div/refusesAStringSubject
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | div 2 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/div/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/div/divisor
  |~~ The number the subject is divided by. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/div/divisor/refusesAString
  |~~ A string divisor is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | div "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/div/divisor/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/div/divisor/NotNumberError
  |~~ The divisor is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/div/divisor/ZeroError
  |~~ The divisor is not zero: a division by zero has no finite answer,
      and a divisor that may be zero is tested first,
      `if (/count | eq 0) ~(0) ~(divOf /sum /count)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(eq 0 | not)

:number/div/divisor/ZeroError/refusesZero
  |~~ A division by zero is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | div 0 !| type | eq ::number/div/divisor/ZeroError)

:number/div/answer
  |~~ The subject divided by the divisor. ~~|
  ::number

:number/round
  |~~ The number rounded half away from zero to the digits after the
      point the call gives, none when it gives none; a negative count
      rounds to tens and beyond. A string holding a number is no number:
      `parseJson` reads one out of it first.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/round/keepsTheDigitsGiven
  |~~ Two digits keep two places after the point. ~~|
  ::law~(3.14159 | round 2 | eq 3.14)

:number/round/roundsAHalfAwayFromZero
  |~~ A half rounds away from zero. ~~|
  ::law~(2.5 | round | eq 3)

:number/round/roundsANegativeHalfAwayFromZero
  |~~ A negative half rounds away from zero too. ~~|
  ::law~(-2.5 | round | eq -3)

:number/round/roundsToHundreds
  |~~ A negative count rounds to tens and beyond. ~~|
  ::law~(1234 | round -2 | eq 1200)

:number/round/printsNoNegativeZero
  |~~ A negative number rounded to zero prints as 0. ~~|
  ::law~(-0.4 | round | print | eq "0")

:number/round/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no number to round. ~~|
  ::law~("2.5" | round !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/round/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/round/digits
  |~~ The digits after the point, a whole number, none when the call
      gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0 0)

:number/round/digits/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count of digits is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError
  |~~ The digits of `round` are a whole number, `round 2`. ~~|

:number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ Half a digit is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(2.5 | round 0.5 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError ::number])

:number/round/answer
  |~~ The number rounded to the digits. ~~|
  ::number

:number/gt
  |~~ Whether the subject is greater than the number the call gives. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/gt/comparesNumbers
  |~~ Ten is greater than five. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | gt 5 | eq true)

:number/gt/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/gt/than/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/gt/answer
  |~~ Whether the subject comes after the value compared. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/gte
  |~~ Whether the subject is greater than or equal to the number the
      call gives.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/gte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A number is greater than or equal to itself. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | gte 10 | eq true)

:number/gte/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/gte/than/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/gte/answer
  |~~ Whether the subject comes after the value compared or equals it. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/lt
  |~~ Whether the subject is less than the number the call gives. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/lt/comparesNumbers
  |~~ Five is less than ten. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | lt 10 | eq true)

:number/lt/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/lt/than/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/lt/answer
  |~~ Whether the subject comes before the value compared. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/lte
  |~~ Whether the subject is less than or equal to the number the call
      gives.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/lte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A number is less than or equal to itself. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | lte 10 | eq true)

:number/lte/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/lte/than/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/lte/answer
  |~~ Whether the subject comes before the value compared or equals it. ~~|
  ::boolean
